Z||000000||Richard Parnell||06-08-2001||02:18 PM||||Reading the spoiler post I saw the mention of which front fascia is more efficient.  I've personally always like the '87 coupe front ends' looks much better than the aero front.  I also personally think it is more efficient with the air dam it has.  What does everyone else think? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DecadenceR||06-08-2001||02:19 PM||||I definitely think it looks 100% better...<BR>That's why I was going to update the facia on my 84.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||06-08-2001||02:21 PM||||I think it looks a lot like the C5 'vette.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-08-2001||02:33 PM||||I think it looks a lot like the 'vette nose, too.<BR>Just wish I could get an air dam like the one made for the 84-86 nose.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||06-08-2001||03:30 PM||||The '87-'88 coupe nose looks even more like a Vette with the fog light modification I saw at Carlisle:<BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=34301&a=12993236&p=49175617&Sequence=0&res=high" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=34301&a=12993236&p=49175617&Sequence=0&res=high<BR></A> <P>I was the one that stated that the '87-'88 coupe nose is the most aerodynamic nose in the other post.  I've got the numbers to back it up, but I'm too lazy to look them up right now.<P>My specs are only for stock parts though.  I think that those that have the aftermarket front air dam on the GT aero nose may solve the biggest problem of that nose....the slight lift due to the upward angled parts of the nose on each side of the bottom-most lower radiator opening (but it's a reasonable compromise since those with the air dam know it's way to easy to hit a curb with it though).  The lift numbers of the aero nose is still no where near the poor specs of the '84-'86 nose.<P>There's still more scrub (surface) area on the aero nose of a GT then there is on a '87-'88 coupe nose though. The GT nose requires the air to move across a larger surface area and thus creates more friction. A small difference but every little bit counts.<P>The most slippery Fiero aerodynamically would actually be a GT fastback with an '87-'88 coupe nose and no wing.  The wing actually creates a slight amount of drag on the GT because the slightly higher decklid of the GT puts it more into the airstream and the sail panel windows on the GT fastback solve most of the turbulance problem present on the notchback Fieros that the wing is really meant to solve.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
